  "result": "Not quite. A language model remembers nothing on its own between conversations, so your assistant won't recall last week's chat unless you build that in.\n\nHere's what it looks like. Last week, Sam told the assistant, \"I finished the laser cutter training.\" This week Sam opens a new chat and asks, \"Can I book the laser cutter?\" The model only sees what's in front of it right now. Sam's new message is all it gets, so it has no idea about the training and asks again.\n\nTo fix that, your code has to do the remembering:\n1. At the end of a session, your code saves a short note, like `{\"sam\": [\"laser cutter training: done\"]}`, to a file or database.\n2. At the start of the next session, your code loads that note and puts it into the text the model reads.\n3. Now the model \"knows\" Sam is trained, but only because you handed it the note.\n\nThis matters for your budget too. Everything you reload costs tokens on every request, so you'll want to save short notes rather than whole transcripts.\n\nThat's exactly the \"what it saves between sessions\" decision from your mission, and it's coming up next. Quick check: in the Sam example, who actually remembered the training, the model or your code?",
